  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">AHP</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">two WRO partners in the Gaza Strip (Aid and Hope Program for Cancer Patient Care(AHP), and Wefaq Society For Women and Child Care) on developing further their approaches on community based mental health . Both WROs are  operating in two different communities in Gaza with a high prevalence of common mental health issues amongst women and adolescent girls. Under this intervention each WROs willestablish a community group that consists of 20 women (the group will include: WRO staff members, influencers from the community, key women, and women and girls who experienced MH/ psycho-social counselling) and adolescent girls, in total we will have two groups that have 40 members from the two targeted communities. The consultant will support the two WROs and the two groups of women and adolescent girls from the two targeted communities (to understand, critically interrogate existing community-based mental health care approaches, and systematically co-create mental health care actions that is suitable for the communities.  Such process will adopt culturally-rooted concepts that can be easily understood and communicated in local  language. [9:37 AM] Sukaina Samhan
Based on the cocreation of the manuals, the consultants will provide training and coaching session tothe WROs and the established groups. Further, the consultant will help the WROs to learn, document, and advocate for the integration of mental health care actions in the development and women&#x2019;s empowerment programs.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Aid and Hope Program for Cancer Patients Care (AHP) is a Palestinian nongovernmental organization that was established in 2010 in the Gaza Strip. AHP was the first of its kind in Gaza to provide awareness raising and psychosocial support for breast cancer patients. During our 11 years of operation, we have managed to reach over 120,000 women, approximately 70 percent of whom were unaware of ways to ensure early detection or screen for breast cancer.

AHP aims to increase awareness of breast cancer in the Gaza community, improve women&#x2019;s access to early diagnostic facilities through clinical breast examination, facilitate the referral of at-risk women to mammographic screening services, and provide women with navigators who will engage in patient advocacy and increase and facilitatecommunication between patients, health care providers, and family members.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Aid and Hope</narrative>
  </participating-org>
